WHY THE KEYS RING IS A SOCKET: it has the property the memfd was believed
to have, and the two facts the access control is built out of.
  ok   a socket cannot be opened through /proc/<pid>/fd at all
  ok   and the kernel says so by name: ENXIO, not a permission accident
  ok   an abstract name binds
  ok   and a SECOND bind of it is EADDRINUSE: first binder wins, no unlink
  ok   SO_PASSCRED is accepted on it
  ok   and every datagram arrives with SCM_CREDENTIALS attached
  ok   carrying the KERNEL's answer for the sender's pid, which it cannot forge

AND THE ONE THIS FILE CANNOT CLOSE, WHICH IS NOW CLOSED SOMEWHERE ELSE:
  /proc/sys/kernel/yama/ptrace_scope = 0 on this DEV HOST.
  A same-uid attacker PTRACE_ATTACHes the victim and reads its memory
  directly -- no userland mechanism prevents that, and every window
  system on Linux has the same exposure.  It is a DISTRIBUTION setting,
  and user/linuxinit.ad now sets kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=1 as PID 1
  and reads it back.  It is NOT set on this dev host (that would be a
  change to somebody's working machine, and ptrace_scope is not
  namespaced, so no unprivileged harness can set it either), which is
  why the ptrace=0 above is still the honest reading HERE and why the
  measurement of the setting lives in a real boot:
      tests/linux/ptrace_scope_boot.sh   -- 6 assertions, one of them
      this exact attack run on this host (attach SUCCEEDS, scope 0)
      and inside a boot of this tree (REFUSED, scope 1).

